## Rust reproducer

```rust
pub trait It {
    fn count(&self) -> usize {
        let f = || 0usize;
        f()
    }
}

pub struct Counter;

impl It for Counter {}
```

## Output 


```lean
def call
  {Self : Type} (ItInst : It Self) (c : It.count.closure Self) (_ : Unit) :
  Result Std.Usize
  := do
  ok 0#usize

def Counter.Insts.count
  (self : Counter) : Result Std.Usize := do
  call
    Counter.Insts.It () ()

def Counter.Insts.It : It Counter := {
  count := Counter.Insts.count
}
```

The closure is pulled out and takes the iterator instance as a parameter (even though it is unused). Then the definition of `count` has to pass an instance when it calls `f`, and it passes the instance that is going to be defined (which is invalid lean). The closure transformation is done by charon so we might want to move this to a charon issue.

This:

```
fn h(x: &str) {}

fn main() {
    {
        if true && true {
            h("foo")
        } else { 
            h("bar")
        }
