Notice how the `const("bar")` appears twice, meaning that the else-branch is duplicated. The desired outcome is for no such duplication to occur.

This is throwing Eurydice off the rails in a non-recoverable way. I'll elaborate once I file an issue on the Eurydice side.

Currently, there is no way to tell apart a function that has no body because it's external from a function that has no body because it couldn't be translated. Furthermore, external function names inherit the name of the module they're defined in, e.g. here:

```rust
// file foo.rs
fn bar() {
    unsafe extern "C" {
        fn foo(); // full name: foo::bar::foo()
    }

    unsafe { foo() }
}

fn main() {
    unsafe extern "C" {
        fn foo(); // full name: foo::main::foo()
    }

    unsafe { foo() };
    bar();
}
```

This shouldn't be the case, I think -- extern functions are identified by name and all map to the "same" function from what i understand, i.e. both foo functions should be the same and have the name `foo`. Them being the same exactly is probably not too much of a big deal (at least for me), however the name problem is quite bothersome. 

In particular, `alloc::Global` uses [the extern functions `__rust_alloc`, `__rust_dealloc`, etc.](https://doc.rust-lang.org/src/alloc/alloc.rs.html#11-38) to do dynamic allocations, but if one wants to stub these functions in their Charon client it is quite annoying:
1. their name is e.g. `std::alloc::__rust_alloc`, making using name matching not very reliable (e.g. a user could just re-specify `__rust_alloc` in their own module, where the name pattern wouldn't match) 
2. there is no way to know these functions are `extern`, so if a user were to create a function that happens to be called `__rust_alloc`, it might get accidentally stubbed, despite having nothing to do with the magic `__rust_alloc` function.

Adding e.g. `is_extern: bool` to `FunDecl` would fix this :)

Similar to #176, this is a request to support mutually recursive functions and trait implementations.

For instance, I hit it with a recursive call via `FnOnce`:

```rust
fn call(f: impl FnOnce()) {
    f()
}

fn f(b: bool) {
    if b {
        call(|| f(false));
    }
}
```

fails to translate with:

```text
[Error] Detected groups of mixed mutually recursive definitions (such as a type mutually recursive with a function, or a function mutually recursive with a trait implementation):

# Group 1:
- fun decl(Source: 'src/lib.rs', lines 7:0-11:1): c::f
- trait impl(Source: 'src/lib.rs', lines 9:13-9:24): c::f::{impl core::ops::function::FnOnce<(), ()> for c::f::closure}
- fun decl(Source: 'src/lib.rs', lines 9:13-9:24): c::f::{impl core::ops::function::FnOnce<(), ()> for c::f::closure}::call_once

Compiler source: interp/Interp.ml, line 103
```

I also hit a form with custom traits as well:

```rust
trait Trait {
    fn f(&self, b: bool);
}

struct S;

fn call<T: Trait>(v: &T, b: bool) {
    T::f(v, b)
}

impl S {
    fn f(&self, b: bool) {
        if b {
            call(self, false)
        }
    }
}

impl Trait for S {
    fn f(&self, b: bool) {
        self.f(b)
    }
}
```

