I got claude to make me a minimal repro, apologies if the following feels a bit AI-generated, because it is.

-----

Aeneas fails with a generic `[Error] Unimplemented` when translating a function that holds a **shared borrow of a value whose type contains a reference** (e.g. `&self` where `Self` stores a `&'a T` field) **and** makes an intermediate call whose result is bound (e.g. via `?`) rather than tail-returned.

This is a manifestation of the nested-borrow limitation (umbrella #181), but it differs from the existing reports in two ways worth a dedicated MWE:

1. The function **signature translates fine** — there is no "Nested borrows are not supported yet (found in the signature of …)" error (cf. #464) and no `InterpBorrows.ml` "Found a case of unsupported nested borrows" (cf. #929). The failure happens during symbolic execution of the **body**, reported only as `Unimplemented`.
2. The trigger is an extremely common pattern — a struct that *stores* a reference, with a `&self` method that uses `?` — so the unclear error is easy to hit and hard to diagnose.

## Minimal reproducer

```rust
pub struct S<'a> {
    pub r: &'a u8,
}

fn f(x: u8) -> Result<u8, ()> {
    Ok(x)
}

impl<'a> S<'a> {
    // FAILS: "[Error] Unimplemented"
    pub fn m(&self, x: u8) -> Result<u8, ()> {
        let y = f(x)?;
        Ok(y)
    }
}
```

```
[Error] Unimplemented
Source: 'src/lib.rs', lines <m-body>
[Warn ] Could not translate the body of function '…::{…::S<'a>}::m because of previous error
Compiler source: interp/Interp.ml, line 550
```

(`Interp.ml:550` is the `CFailure` catch-all reporter in `evaluate_function_symbolic`; the real craise is deeper, in the abstraction-ending machinery reached while the `&self` borrow over the inner `&'a u8` is live across the `f(x)` call's region abstraction.)

## What isolates the trigger (controls that DO translate)

All three of these translate cleanly — only `m` above fails:

```rust
// (1) Struct OWNS its data (no reference under &self):
pub struct Owned { pub r: u8 }
impl Owned { pub fn ok_owned(&self, x: u8) -> Result<u8, ()> { let y = f(x)?; Ok(y) } }

// (2) Same ref-storing struct, but the call is TAIL-returned (no intermediate bind):
impl<'a> S<'a> { pub fn ok_tailcall(&self, x: u8) -> Result<u8, ()> { f(x) } }

// (3) Same ref-storing struct, but `self` is taken BY VALUE (cf. the supported
//     adt-borrows.rs `SharedWrapper::unwrap(self)`):
impl<'a> S<'a> { pub fn ok_byval(self, x: u8) -> Result<u8, ()> { let y = f(x)?; Ok(y) } }
```

So the necessary conditions are: (a) a **shared borrow** of a value whose type contains a reference (here `&self`, but a free `fn g(s: &S<'a>, …)` fails identically), **and** (b) an **intermediate call** whose result is bound and used afterward (so the call's abstraction must be ended while that borrow is still live). `adt-borrows.rs` passes because its `&'a T`-storing wrappers only use **by-value** `self` methods.

## Impact

This blocks essentially every method of a borrow-heavy protocol type. Concretely it blocks the entire `libcrux-psq` ProVerif extraction via the Aeneas pipeline: `Responder<'a, Rng>`, `QueryInitiator<'a>`, `ResponderCiphersuite<'a>` (`kex: &'a DHKeyPair`), etc. all store references and have `&self`/`&mut self` methods using `?`, so every protocol method translates to an error stub.

## Environment

- aeneas `cryspen/aeneas@6a3fc35b` (a fork off an older `AeneasVerif/aeneas` main; the failure is in core `Interp`/symbolic execution and reproduces with plain `-backend lean`, not just `-dump-pure-ir`).
- charon `1ec8d4bb` (`nightly-2026.05.14`, v0.1.196), `--preset=aeneas`.
- rustc `nightly-2026-02-07`.

Please confirm against current `AeneasVerif/aeneas` main; the umbrella is #181 (nested borrows in function signatures), but this body-execution manifestation with the opaque `Unimplemented` error and a struct-stored-ref + `?` trigger appears untracked (closest: #929, a different repro/error in `InterpBorrows.ml`).

