`Option::map`, `Option::is_some_and`, `bool::then` and `Result::unwrap_or` had no models, so Aeneas emitted an axiom for each use. `tests/lean/Issue767AdtConstructor.lean` had one for `Option::map`.

This adds models for the four methods, following their Rust implementations:

- `Option::map`, `Option::is_some_and` and `bool::then` call the closure through its `FnOnce` instance, as `Result::map_err` does. Each comes with a spec in the style of `loop.spec`: an arbitrary postcondition, plus one hypothesis per case, and the closure only has to satisfy it on the case where it is called.
- `Result::unwrap_or` is a pure `step_pure_def` model, like `Option::unwrap_or`.

Tests are `#[verify::test]` functions in `tests/src/options.rs`:

- each method on both of its cases;
- closures that panic, to check they are not called on `None` or `false`;
- a closure that captures a value, so its state is not empty;
- `unwrap_or` on `Ok` and on `Err`, through a helper that fixes the error type, because of #1018.

`ExtractBuiltinLean.ml` is regenerated with `make extract-lean-std`. Regenerating all tests with `IN_CI=1` changes only `Options.lean` and `Issue767AdtConstructor.lean`, where the model replaces the `Option::map` axiom.

@oliver-butterley's `std-lib-models` branch also has a model of `Result::unwrap_or`.

